什么？

16
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.020
We look gorgeous today.
我们今天看起来美极了。

17
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.040
You all should know.
你们都该知道。

18
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.900
I don't know what this podcast is going to be today, but we look hot.
我不知道今天的播客会是什么样，但我们看起来真的很辣。

19
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.520
Well, Jenna has that like dark, smoky eyeliner.
珍娜画了那种深邃的烟熏眼妆。

20
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.140
I did a smoky eye today.
我今天画了烟熏妆。

21
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.560
How do you even do that?
你是怎么画出来的？

22
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.760
I need a tutorial.
我需要教程。

23
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.620
Oh, multiple YouTube tutorials is how we got this smoky eye today.
噢，我看了好几个YouTube教程才画出今天的烟熏妆。

24
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.320
I did super straight hair.
我把头发拉得特别直。

25
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.540
You look amazing.
你看起来棒极了。

26
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.580
You look like you're about to take over like multiple corporations.
你看上去就像准备要接管好几家大公司一样。

27
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.960
I am wearing a blazer.
我穿了件西装外套。

28
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:07.980
I know.
我知道。

29
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.920
I guess we have to say that because people can't see us, but we look amazing.
我想我们得说出来，因为大家看不见，但我们确实看起来棒极了。

30
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.720
Angela, your nails look incredible.
安吉拉，你的指甲看起来太赞了。

31
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:15.840
I mean, details.
我是说，细节。

32
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.900
We have details.
我们很注重细节。

33
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:17.900
We look good, guys.
我们看起来很棒，伙计们。

34
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.200
We're feeling it today.
我们今天感觉很棒。

35
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.260
We cannot wait to talk about this episode.
我们迫不及待想聊聊这一集了。

36
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.980
And then we have a photo shoot.
之后我们还要去拍照片。

37
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.100
Jenna, who are we?
珍娜，我们是谁？

38
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:24.820
What is happening?
发生了什么？

39
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.140
We're a big deal.
我们可是大人物。

40
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:27.780
I don't know.
我不知道。

41
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.620
Will you tell my kids that next time we're at Target?
下次我们在塔吉特百货时，你能跟我孩子们说一声吗？

42
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.840
Hey, hey.
嘿，嘿。

43
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.820
Big deal over there in the seasonal aisle.
季节性商品区的大人物在那儿呢。

44
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.480
Yeah, and you can only pick one of those things.
没错，而且你只能选其中一样。

45
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:38.320
Pick one.
选一个吧。

46
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.520
You're not getting all three.
你不能三个全要。

47
00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.040
Let's get to talking about what we're here for.
让我们开始聊聊我们今天的主题吧。

48
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:51.100
It's season five, episode 23, written by the amazing Justin Spitzer and directed by Gene
这是第五季第23集，由了不起的贾斯汀·斯皮策编剧，吉恩·斯图普尼茨基执导。

49
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:51.920
Stupnitsky.
他是这一集的署名导演，但他其实还有个搭档。

50
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.140
He's the credited director on this one, but he actually had a partner.
他是这一集的署名导演，但他其实还有个搭档。

51
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:03.120
This episode was co-directed by Gene Stupnitsky and his longtime writing partner, Lee Eisenberg.
本集由吉恩·斯图普尼茨基和他的长期写作搭档李·艾森伯格共同执导。

52
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.000
That's right.
没错。

53
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.300
They would go on to direct many more episodes together of The Office.
他们后来又一起执导了许多集《办公室》。

54
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.360
They shared a little bit in the commentary that they would rotate who would take credit.
他们在评论音轨里稍微提过，他们会轮流署名。

55
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.340
It sort of went along some DGA rules at the time.
这在当时算是符合美国导演工会的一些规定。

56
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.500
So Gene got credit for this one, but they were both there together every day.
所以这一集署名的是吉恩，但他们俩每天都在现场。

57
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.240
So we want to give Lee a shout out.
所以我们要向李致敬。

58
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.160
And then the next time there's an episode up that says Lee directed, know that Gene was
下次如果看到哪一集写着李执导，要知道吉恩也

59
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.140
right there by his side.
就在他身边。

60
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.160
And we reached out to both of them, and they shared a ton of great details for this episode.
我们联系了他们俩，他们为这一集分享了大量精彩的细节。

61
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.120
So I'm very, very excited.
所以我非常、非常兴奋。

62
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.920
Here's your summary.
这是剧情梗概。

63
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.080
Michael, Pam, and Ryan are in their new office.
迈克尔、帕姆和瑞安在他们的新办公室里。

64
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.200
They're struggling to adjuSt.
他们正努力适应。

65
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.880
It's a very tiny, intimate work environment.
那是一个非常狭小、私密的工作环境。

66
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.260
Very tiny.
非常狭小。

67
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.840
Can I tell you one thing?
我能告诉你一件事吗？

68
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.400
Justin said they even considered finding a smaller room.
贾斯汀说他们甚至考虑过找一个更小的房间。

69
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:51.880
What?
什么？

70
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.640
Can you imagine?
你能想象吗？

71
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:59.000
I can't because I was there, and I know how almost impossible it was to shoot in that room.
我无法想象，因为我当时在场，我知道在那个房间里拍摄几乎是不可能的。

72
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.520
With like the camera operators, the sound department, you guys.
还要加上摄像师、录音部门，还有你们这些人。

73
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.020
Smaller room.
更小的房间。

74
00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.020
That's bonkers to me.
这对我来说太疯狂了。

75
00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.980
Well, upstairs in Dunder Mifflin, there is a new receptionist in town.
好吧，在邓德·米夫林楼上，来了一位新前台。

76
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.340
A new lady named Kelly Erin Hannon.
一位名叫凯莉·艾琳·汉农的新女士。

77
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:22.040
Dwight and Andy are both kind of crushing on her, but they also have this new friendship.
德怀特和安迪都有点迷恋她，但他们之间也建立了新的友谊。

78
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.600
So this crush is going to really challenge their new bond.
所以这份迷恋将真正考验他们新建立的纽带。

79
00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.480
In the meantime, Jim is trying to figure out what the heck is a rundown?
与此同时，吉姆正试图弄清楚到底什么是“工作汇报”？

80
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.000
What is a rundown?
什么是工作汇报？

81
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.120
Poor guy.
可怜的家伙。

82
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.260
And let's not forget, there's going to be some giant square pancakes.
别忘了，还有一些巨大的方形煎饼。

83
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:39.240
Yeah.
没错。

84
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.900
Lady, I forgot that they were big squares.
天哪，我都忘了它们是巨大的方形。

85
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.900
I have details on that.
我有关于那个的细节。

86
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:44.480
You do?
真的吗？

87
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:44.780
I do.
真的。

88
00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:45.620
Oh, I can't wait.
噢，我等不及了。

89
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.120
All right.
好吧。

90
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.380
I have some pancake details.
我有一些关于煎饼的细节。

91
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:48.060
I can't wait.
我等不及了。

92
00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.360
Let's get into some fast facts.
让我们进入一些快速事实环节。

93
00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:49.960
All right.
好的。

94
00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.340
Here's a mini fast fact.
这是一个迷你快速事实。

95
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.180
This episode actually aired the same night as Dream Team.
这一集实际上和《梦之队》在同一晚播出。

96
00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:01.080
In between these two episodes, they ran the very first episode of Parks and Recreation.
在这两集之间，他们播出了《公园与游憩》的第一集。

97
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.300
How about that?
那又如何呢？

98
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.340
We were like a little sandwich.
我们就像一个小三明治。

99
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.860
We were, and we were the bread, and Parks and Recreation was the meat.
确实，我们是面包，而《公园与游憩》是肉。

100
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:08.640
Bologna.
博洛尼亚香肠。

101
00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:09.940
The cheese?
奶酪？

102
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:10.440
Cheese.
奶酪。

103
00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.520
Sounds like a dig.
听起来像是在挖苦人。

104
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:12.400
We don't mean that.
我们不是那个意思。

105
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.460
No, I didn't mean it to be.
不，我没那个意思。

106
00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.100
I had a bologna sandwich every day in my lunch growing up.
我从小到大午餐每天都吃博洛尼亚香肠三明治。

107
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.720
No, but it's, you know, saying something is bologna or something is cheese.
不是，我的意思是，你知道，说某样东西是博洛尼亚香肠或者奶酪什么的。

108
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.400
It seems like a dig, but...
听起来像是在挖苦，但是……

109
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.160
They were the meat.
它们是肉。

110
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.560
Well, now that seems like a dig on the bread.
好吧，现在听起来像是在挖苦面包了。

111
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:27.780
Well, you can't.
好吧，你不能这样。

112
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.620
As if the bread isn't important.
好像面包不重要似的。

113
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.060
Well, the bread's the best part.
面包才是最好吃的部分。

114
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.680
That's just because I love bread.
那只是因为我爱吃面包。

115
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.600
Okay, we'll leave this analogy.
好吧，我们跳过这个比喻。

116
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:36.740
There you have it.
就是这样。

117
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.800
It was office, Parks and Rec office.
那是办公室，公园与游憩的办公室。

118
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:39.300
Yes.
是的。

119
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:44.820
Fast fact number one, I thought we should talk a little bit about this teeny tiny space
第一个冷知识，我觉得我们应该聊聊这个极小的空间

120
00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.180
of Michael Scott Paper Company.
迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司。

121
00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:53.820
This 165 square foot office with no windows on the bottom floor of Dunder Mifflin.
这个位于邓德·米夫林底层、没有窗户的165平方英尺的办公室。

122
00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.920
With a exposed shower in the corner?
角落里还有一个裸露的淋浴间？

123
00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.140
Yes, correct.
是的，没错。

124
00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.480
Now, that was a fakie shower.
那个淋浴间是假的。

125
00:04:59.480 --> 00:04:59.980
Oh.
哦。

126
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.880
That was a design by our set designer, Michael Gallenberg.
那是我们的布景设计师迈克尔·加伦伯格设计的。

127
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.580
Because there was a real shower down there.
因为楼下确实有一个真正的淋浴间。

128
00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:07.700
I had to use it one time.
我有一次不得不去用它。

129
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.500
Not in that room.
不是在那个房间里。

130
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.080
Across the hallway, there was an office that had a tiny shower.
在走廊对面，有一个带小淋浴间的办公室。

131
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.020
Really?
真的吗？

132
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:14.800
Yes.
是的。

133
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.680
Why did you have to use it?
你为什么非得用它？

134
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:17.440
Okay.
好吧。

135
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.500
Well, do you remember the summer my hair turned green?
你还记得我头发变绿的那个夏天吗？

136
00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:20.960
Yep.
记得。

137
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.700
You could really see it on camera.
在镜头里真的看得出来。

138
00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.180
Guys, I spent the whole summer swimming with the kids in the pool and my hair turned green.
伙计们，那个夏天我一直和孩子们在泳池里游泳，结果我的头发变绿了。

139
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.800
It happens to the blondies out there.
金发碧眼的人都会遇到这种情况。

140
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.380
And Kim put this thing on my hair to, like, kind of tint it, you know?
金给我头发上涂了点东西，想给它上点色，你懂的？

141
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.420
To get the green out?
为了把绿色洗掉？

142
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:36.520
To get the green out.
为了把绿色洗掉。

143
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.960
And I had to wash it out of my hair.
我得把它从头发上洗掉。

144
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.180
Like, you put it on and then I had to rinse it out.
就是涂上去，然后我得把它冲洗干净。

145
00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.380
And then she had to blow dry my hair.
然后她得帮我吹干头发。

146
00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:42.980
Okay.
好吧。

147
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.140
Now, why didn't they just use the little sink in the hair and makeup trailer or the shower in your trailer?
那他们为什么不用化妆间里的小水槽，或者你房车里的淋浴间呢？

148
00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:53.220
Because the sink in the hair and makeup trailer, first of all, it didn't get hot.
因为化妆间里的水槽，首先，它不出热水。

149
00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:03.200
And Kim was a little concerned because the water is like this sort of recycled water that it would strip the color off my hair that she was trying to put in.
而且金有点担心，因为那里的水是循环水，会把她努力帮我染上去的颜色洗掉。

150
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:09.480
Now that you say that, I know that because there have been times when I've needed to shower off.
你这么一说我就知道了，因为有时候我也需要冲个澡。

151
00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.560
Your trailers have a shower.
你们的拖车里有淋浴间。

152
00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.260
But it is exactly that.
但它其实就是那样。

153
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.940
It's like a tank of weird sort of chemically treated water.
就像是一个装了某种奇怪化学处理水的储水箱。

154
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:17.320
Right.
没错。

155
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.500
So that it can stay in the tank and then not be poisonous.
这样水才能留在箱子里，而且不会有毒。

156
00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:21.040
Yeah.
是啊。

157
00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:23.260
And it doesn't get very hot.
而且水温也不会很热。

158
00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.040
And also, I've had people tell me not to use it.
另外，还有人跟我说别用那个淋浴间。

159
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.860
I sort of was like, maybe just get rid of the showers and the trailers.
我当时就在想，或许干脆把拖车里的淋浴间拆了算了。

160
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:31.580
Yeah, put in a closet.
对，改成衣帽间。

161
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:32.320
They're taking up space.
它们太占地方了。

162
00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:33.720
We all need a closet.
我们都需要一个衣帽间。

163
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.240
Always need more closet.
永远都需要更多的衣帽间。

164
00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:36.960
I mean, it's part of my life mantra.
我是说，这是我的人生信条之一。

165
00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:38.200
Can I have more closets?
我能多要几个衣帽间吗？

166
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.340
Well, you need closets for your baskets, lady.
好吧，你确实需要衣帽间来放你的篮子，女士。

167
00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:42.880
I do.
确实。

168
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.760
Your basket collection.
你那堆篮子收藏。

169
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:44.920
Give me all the baskets.
把所有的篮子都给我吧。

170
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:50.060
Well, listen, this little room, it was located in our writer's building.
听着，这个小房间，它位于我们的编剧大楼里。

171
00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.520
It was on the bottom floor.
它在一楼。

172
00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.260
And it was an actual storage closet.
而且它真的是个储物间。

173
00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.000
It was not a set.
它不是布景。

174
00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.660
So the walls did not move.
所以墙壁是不能移动的。

175
00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.220
They talked about, should we build Michael Scott Paper Company in the warehouse?
他们当时讨论过，我们要不要在仓库里搭建迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司的场景？

176
00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.460
That was my thought.
我当时也是这么想的。

177
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.460
Where we've built many a set.
我们在那里搭建过很多布景。

178
00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:08.520
No.
不行。

179
00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.720
For whatever reason, they crammed us into the post-production storage room,
不管出于什么原因，他们把我们塞进了后期制作的储藏室里，

180
00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:20.700
where previous to us shooting in there, they had stored thousands of hours of archived
在我们去那里拍摄之前，他们在那儿存放了数千小时的

181
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:21.980
tape of our show.
我们节目的存档录像带。

182
00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.100
This was back in the day.
那是很久以前的事了。

183
00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:29.940
And we just had walls and walls of five seasons of tape on our show.
我们周围全是五季节目以来的录像带墙。

184
00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.340
They had to relocate all the tapes.
他们不得不把所有的录像带都搬走。

185
00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.140
They had to redress the room.
他们不得不重新布置那个房间。

186
00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.020
I said, the shower is fake, but those pipes were real.
我说过，淋浴间是假的，但那些管道是真的。

187
00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.340
There really were these giant pipes.
那里确实有这些巨大的管道。

188
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:40.680
Yes.
是的。

189
00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.400
The big black pipes that were coming out, those were real.
那些伸出来的黑色大管道，它们是真的。

190
00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:44.000
Yes.
没错。

191
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:50.220
Also, the bathrooms that are outside in the hallway, the two doors on either side of the
还有，走廊外面的洗手间，就是迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司门两侧的那两扇门，

192
00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.120
Michael Scott Paper Company door, those bathrooms were real.
那些洗手间是真的。

193
00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.620
Those were the actual working bathrooms for anybody who worked on the bottom floor.
那是所有在一楼工作的人实际使用的洗手间。

194
00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.240
Those were the bathrooms we would use in between scenes when we filmed in the warehouse.
我们在仓库拍摄间隙用的就是那些洗手间。

195
00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:01.680
Yes.
是的。

196
00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.640
Or in that hallway by the editing bay.
或者是在剪辑室旁边的那个走廊里。

197
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:09.020
Right across from us were our two editors, Claire and Dave.
我们的对面就是我们的两位剪辑师，克莱尔和戴夫。

198
00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.220
And above us were the writers' offices.
我们的楼上是编剧办公室。

199
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.300
So we were over in that building with the writers.
所以我们和编剧们在那栋楼里。

200
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:21.380
Also in this building is the lobby area that leads out into the parking lot.
这栋楼里还有通往停车场的大厅区域。

201
00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.680
This room also gets repurposed for Cafe Disco.
这个房间后来也被改造成了“咖啡迪斯科”。

202
00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.220
Yes.
是的。

203
00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.960
We went back down there for that.
我们当时又回到了那里。

204
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.400
So those are all my details on that room.
关于那个房间的情况我大概就介绍到这儿。

205
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.940
I'll have more to share as we break down the episode.
等我们拆解这一集的时候，我会有更多内容分享。

206
00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.080
More little quirks of working in there.
还有更多在那里工作时发现的小怪癖。

207
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:34.500
Yeah.
没错。

208
00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.980
You spent a lot of time in there.
你在那儿待了很长时间。

209
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:36.460
We did.
确实。

210
00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.100
Are you ready for fast fact number two?
准备好听第二个冷知识了吗？

211
00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:39.960
I am.
准备好了。

212
00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:47.460
Remember last week when I told you that Michael sent a letter to everybody through NBC.com
还记得上周我跟你说迈克尔通过NBC.com给每个人发了一封信吗？

213
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:47.680
Yes.
记得。

214
00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:49.560
Announcing his new paper company?
宣布他成立了新的造纸公司？

215
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:58.700
Well, this week he unveiled his website and NBC created a Michael Scott paper company website
嗯，这周他发布了他的网站，NBC还专门制作了一个迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司的网站，

216
00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.940
and it had photos of the room.
上面还有那个房间的照片。

217
00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.500
It was basically the same room taken from three different angles.
基本上就是从三个不同角度拍的同一个房间。

218
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:11.860
It had the downloadable coupon that is shown in this episode.
上面还有这一集里展示的那张可下载优惠券。

219
00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:15.320
You know, the one that promises unparalleled customer service.
你知道的，就是那张承诺提供无与伦比客户服务的优惠券。

220
00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:15.660
Yep.
没错。

221
00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.200
That Pam and Ryan refused to copy.
帕姆和瑞安拒绝复印的那张。

222
00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:23.320
And it also had the mission statement for Michael Scott paper company.
而且上面还有迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司的使命宣言。

223
00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:24.820
Yes, it did.
确实有。

224
00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.460
And we reached out to Joya.
我们联系了乔亚。

225
00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:27.980
You know, we've talked about Joya before.
你知道的，我们之前提到过乔亚。

226
00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:33.940
She was in charge of all the digital content for the office that was on NBC.com.
她负责NBC.com上所有《办公室》的数字内容。

227
00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.080
She shared with us what the mission statement said.
她跟我们分享了使命宣言的内容。

228
00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.800
Here it is.
内容如下。

229
00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.940
It's the Michael Scott paper company's mission to provide you the highest quality paper,
迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司的使命是为您提供最高质量的纸张，

230
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.360
the best possible service at the lowest possible prices.
以最低的价格提供最好的服务。

231
00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:48.420
Sounds good.
听起来不错。

232
00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:49.440
Just wait.
等着瞧。

233
00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:50.460
There's more.
还有呢。

234
00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.040
This paper business is more than just paper.
这份造纸生意不仅仅是卖纸。

235
00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:54.560
More than just a business.
不仅仅是一门生意。

236
00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:59.040
It's about establishing quality human relationships with our clients.
它是关于与客户建立高质量的人际关系。

237
00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:04.560
While little Timmy wakes up in the middle of the night with whooping cough and you need someone
当小蒂米半夜因百日咳醒来，你需要有人

238
00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.260
to call, we'll be there.
打电话时，我们会陪着你。

239
00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.760
When sweet Christine gets stood up by her prom date, we'll be there.
当可爱的克里斯汀被舞伴放鸽子时，我们会陪着你。

240
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.480
When the dam breaks in your backyard and the only thing that can stop the flow of water
当你后院的大坝决堤，唯一能阻止水流

241
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.180
from flooding your house is a ream of paper, we'll be there.
淹没你家房子的东西就是一令纸时，我们会陪着你。

242
00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.600
At the Michael Scott paper company, we'll always be there.
在迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司，我们永远会陪着你。

243
00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:30.220
Through thick and thin, in sickness and health, till death or retirement do us part, we'll be there.
无论顺境逆境，无论生病健康，直到死亡或退休将我们分开，我们都会陪着你。

244
00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:34.780
For a while, it's just pretty standard.
有一段时间，这看起来还挺标准的。

245
00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:35.780
Kind of boilerplate.
算是那种套话吧。

246
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:36.040
Yeah.
是啊。

247
00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:36.460
Yeah.
没错。

248
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.860
But I really like it when he says we're more than just a business.
但我真的很喜欢他说我们不仅仅是一门生意那段。

249
00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:43.400
We're about quality human relationships with our clients.
我们是关于与客户建立高质量的人际关系。

250
00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:44.320
I'm like, oh.
我当时就想，噢。

251
00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:50.040
And then it goes off into some very specific examples of when you might be there for me.
接着它列举了一些非常具体的例子，说明你可能会在什么时候支持我。

252
00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:50.540
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

253
00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.280
But in the end, I liked it.
但最后，我还是挺喜欢的。

254
00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:56.800
I think I was a little curious about what kind of backyard you have that has a dam.
我其实有点好奇，你家后院得是什么样才能有个水坝啊。

255
00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.020
Well, listen, we'll be there.
好吧，听着，我们会去的。

256
00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.660
Fast fact number three, Ellie Kemper is our new receptioniSt.
第三个小知识，艾莉·坎伯是我们新的前台接待员。

257
00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:07.140
Woo-hoo!
呜呼！

258
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.100
We love Ellie.
我们很喜欢艾莉。

259
00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.320
We are definitely going to have her on the show.
我们绝对会邀请她上节目的。

260
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.860
You know, Ellie and I both grew up in St. Louis.
你知道吗，我和艾莉都是在圣路易斯长大的。

261
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:15.020
I know.
我知道。

262
00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.020
Now, I did not know her growing up.
不过，我小时候并不认识她。

263
00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.840
She is six years younger than me.
她比我小六岁。

264
00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:25.600
I had actually graduated college and moved to Los Angeles while she was still in high school.
我大学毕业搬到洛杉矶的时候，她还在上高中。

265
00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:33.720
I'm six years older than my husband, so you just described our relationship, I guess, had we known each other back then.
我比我丈夫大六岁，所以你刚才描述的，大概就是如果我们当时就认识的话，我们的关系吧。

266
00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:38.440
By the way, do you know who her high school drama teacher was?
顺便问一下，你知道她高中的戏剧老师是谁吗？

267
00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:39.780
Ellie's?
艾莉的吗？

268
00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:40.260
Uh-huh.
嗯哼。

269
00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:40.740
No.
不知道。

270
00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:41.940
Jon Hamm.
乔·哈姆。

271
00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:43.820
Shut the front door!
别开玩笑了！

272
00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:44.120
Yeah.
真的。

273
00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.360
Jon Hamm also grew up in St. Louis.
乔·哈姆也是在圣路易斯长大的。

274
00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.340
I also did not know Jon Hamm growing up, even though we were closer in age.
我小时候也不认识乔·哈姆，尽管我们年龄更接近。

275
00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:55.340
I can't believe that was Ellie's drama teacher.
真不敢相信那是艾莉的戏剧老师。

276
00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:55.700
Yeah.
是啊。

277
00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.700
But, of course, you know, Phyllis was also from St. Louis.
当然，你也知道，菲利斯也是圣路易斯人。

278
00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:00.120
Yes.
是的。

279
00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.200
Our director, Ken Kwapis, was from St. Louis.
我们的导演肯·夸皮斯也是圣路易斯人。

280
00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.060
And our first AD, Rusty Mahmood, was from St. Louis.
还有我们的第一副导演拉斯蒂·马哈茂德，也是圣路易斯人。

281
00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.060
So, we were all very excited.
所以，我们当时都非常兴奋。

282
00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.460
Well, you know, on the DVD commentary, there was a little bit of chatter.
嗯，你知道，在DVD评论音轨里，大家聊过一点。

283
00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:13.760
On the DVD was Jenna, Gene, and Lee, and Justin.
DVD里有珍娜、吉恩、李和贾斯汀。

284
00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:15.280
Nice group.
很棒的组合。

285
00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:16.040
Nice group.
很棒的组合。

286
00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:22.800
There was a little bit of chatter about which city in America was most represented in the cast and crew of The Office.
当时大家聊到《办公室》的演职人员中，哪个美国城市的人数最多。

287
00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.880
I think with the addition of Ellie, it became St. Louis.
我觉得随着艾莉的加入，圣路易斯就成了第一。

288
00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:27.080
It was Boston.
之前是波士顿。

289
00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:28.820
It became St. Louis.
后来变成了圣路易斯。

290
00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:29.480
Yes!
没错！

291
00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:38.080
And Jenna, I want you to know, you have clearly been a, this is going to make me laugh, obsessed with facts for a long time.
珍娜，我想让你知道，你显然一直是个——这会让我笑出来——一直是个沉迷于冷知识的人。

292
00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:42.520
I want you to hear what you brought to the DVD commentary.
我想让你听听你在DVD评论里说了什么。

293
00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:43.920
Gary D.
加里·D。

294
00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:50.320
is also from St. Louis, and so is Shannon, one of our PAs, and Ken Kwapis, one of our directors.
也是圣路易斯人，还有我们的制片助理香农，以及我们的导演之一肯·夸皮斯也是。

295
00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.180
See, you said you were not going to have any fun facts, and now you're just pulling them out.
看吧，你刚才还说你没有冷知识要分享，现在却一个接一个地往外掏。

296
00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:56.580
So many, people are having so much fun with my facts.
太多了，大家对我的冷知识乐在其中呢。

297
00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:02.080
What about Shannon, who was also from St. Louis?
那香农呢，她也是圣路易斯人？

298
00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:02.780
I know.
我知道。

299
00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:04.360
Well, you listed them all.
好吧，你把他们都列出来了。

300
00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:05.960
I sort of just took a snippet out.
我只是截取了一小段而已。

301
00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.840
You sort of talked for a while about St. Louis.
你刚才聊了一会儿圣路易斯。

302
00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:13.900
But clearly, you went into this DVD commentary saying, guys, I hope I have some fun facts.
但很明显，你在录制这张DVD评论音轨时，心里想着：伙计们，希望我能准备些有趣的冷知识。

303
00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.460
So much so that they were like, you do have great facts.
以至于他们都说，你准备的冷知识真的很棒。

304
00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:22.180
And it just cracked me up to hear you talking about your fun facts, and now you have your fast facts.
听到你谈论那些冷知识，我简直笑坏了，现在你又有了“快问快答”。

305
00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:28.040
I'll tell you what, when I would go into DVD commentaries, I was often worried that I wouldn't have anything to talk about.
跟你说实话，以前我去录DVD评论音轨时，常担心自己没话可说。

306
00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:33.340
So I would do kind of the equivalent of a mini deep dive on something.
所以我通常会做些类似小型深度挖掘的工作。

307
00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:43.180
I wanted to come in with like an interesting tidbit that we could throw in there if there was ever a moment where we weren't covering something about the show.
我想准备一些有趣的边角料，万一节目里出现冷场，我们就能拿出来填补空白。

308
00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:44.360
This is why I love you.
这就是我爱你的原因。

309
00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.800
Goes back so far.
这习惯由来已久了。

310
00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.120
It does, but it just got me so tickled.
确实，但这真的把我逗乐了。

311
00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:52.360
We had a fan question from KJ Davey.
我们收到了一位粉丝KJ Davey的提问。

312
00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.100
Erin is my favorite character of The Office.
“艾琳是我在《办公室》里最喜欢的角色。”

313
00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:59.100
How did the producers come up with her personality and her character as a whole?
“制片人是怎么构思出她的个性和整体角色设定的？”

314
00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:04.060
And how much creative liberty did Ellie have in creating her character's quirks and traits?
“艾琳在塑造角色的怪癖和特征时，有多少创作自由？”

315
00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:11.460
KJ, Gene told me something crazy about the development of the character of Kelly Aaron.
KJ，吉恩曾跟我说过一件关于凯利·艾琳这个角色开发的疯狂往事。

316
00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:19.820
He said that the writers had not fully fleshed out Aaron's personality by the time we started shooting.
他说在我们开拍时，编剧们还没完全定型艾琳的性格。

317
00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:28.440
So when we started, they had Ellie perform all of her scenes and all of her talking heads two different ways.
所以刚开始的时候，他们让艾琳把所有的戏份和采访镜头都演了两个版本。

318
00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:30.700
As two different characters.
当作两个完全不同的角色来演。

319
00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:31.840
I remember this.
我记得这件事。

320
00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:36.700
She had to do them in like that earnest, quirky way that we know and love as Aaron.
她得用我们所熟知并喜爱的艾琳那种真诚、古怪的方式演一遍。

321
00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:38.460
That's the one we eventually go with.
那就是我们最终选用的版本。

322
00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:46.860
But Gene said, believe it or not, there exists a bizarro Aaron where her lines are also delivered in a no-nonsense way.
但吉恩说，信不信由你，其实还存在一个“异次元艾琳”，她的台词是用一种一本正经的方式说出来的。

323
00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:52.720
And I asked him, how long did she have to do this before they decided?
我问他，在他们做决定之前，她得这样演多久？

324
00:14:53.360 --> 00:15:01.380
And he said they definitely had like two totally different versions of her talking heads written.
他说他们确实为她的采访镜头写了两个截然不同的版本。

325
00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:09.100
And then in her scenes with Dwight and Andy hitting on her, she had to then perform those with different characteristics.
而且在德怀特和安迪追求她的戏份里，她也必须用不同的性格特征去表演。

326
00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:15.180
I mean, Gene said he gives her so much credit because this was her first big role on a TV show.
吉恩说他非常佩服她，因为这是她在电视剧里的第一个大角色。

327
00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:19.160
And she basically did two versions of Aaron in every scene for the entire week.
她基本上在整整一周的时间里，每一场戏都演了两个版本的艾琳。

328
00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:23.440
So until you shared about that sort of two versions of Aaron, I had forgotten about it.
在你提起那两个版本的艾琳之前，我都快忘了这事了。

329
00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.940
But now there's a deleted scene that makes sense to me.
但现在有个删减片段我终于看懂了。

330
00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:27.720
Aaron just looks at camera.
艾琳只是看着镜头。

331
00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:29.600
You know, she's doing a talking head.
你知道，她当时正在录采访镜头。

332
00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.020
And she's just like, do I have to do one of these on all of my breaks?
她说：“我休息的时候还得录这个吗？”

333
00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:33.780
Yes!
没错！

334
00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:34.780
Angela!
安吉拉！

335
00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.440
James Carey had the candy bag for this episode.
詹姆斯·凯瑞负责这集的糖果袋（素材包）。

336
00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:41.320
And it included Aaron's two talking heads.
里面就包含了艾琳的那两个采访镜头。

337
00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.400
Neither of which end up in the episode.
这两个版本最终都没出现在正片里。

338
00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.300
Well, one of them is on the DVD.
不过其中一个收录在DVD里了。

339
00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:52.740
Okay, this is the one that was in the candy bag as their first choice, which was, it's pretty cool.
好的，这个就是糖果袋里的首选版本，其实挺酷的。

340
00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:54.960
Like any other office, I guess.
“我想这跟其他办公室一样吧。”

341
00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:58.380
Phones, computers, copiers.
“电话、电脑、复印机。”

342
00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.020
Do I have to do this on all my breaks?
“我休息的时候还得录这个吗？”

343
00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:01.600
Yeah!
“没错！”

344
00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.040
Is that the one that's included?
这就是收录进去的那个版本吗？

345
00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.500
Yeah, and I was a little confused because I thought it felt sort of off.
是的，我当时还有点困惑，因为我觉得感觉有点不对劲。

346
00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.400
Then they had this must shoot alternate, which went like this.
然后他们还有个必须拍摄的备选版本，是这样的。

347
00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:14.480
Most places you go, the chairs are really crappy and you're uncomfortable all day.
“大多数地方的椅子都很烂，让你整天都不舒服。”

348
00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.340
My chair here is great.
“我这里的椅子很棒。”

349
00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:18.660
I really hope this works out.
“我真希望这能成。”

350
00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.520
And that was like the one that quirky Aaron would say.
那个版本就是古怪的艾琳会说的话。

351
00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:23.220
Yeah.
是啊。

352
00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:26.400
I guess that's our sign that the office got the new chairs.
我想这就是办公室换了新椅子的信号。

353
00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:28.500
Because she's loving Pam's chair.
因为她很喜欢帕姆的那把椅子。

354
00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:29.320
That's it.
就是这样。

355
00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:33.300
I think Pam finally got her new chair and now she's working down in Michael Scott Paper Company.
我想帕姆终于换了新椅子，结果她现在却在迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司工作。

356
00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:35.600
Pam talks about the chairs too later on.
帕姆后来也提到了椅子。

357
00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:36.100
I know.
我知道。

358
00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:42.680
I want to say that this doing two versions of your character happened to me on the film Blades of Glory.
我想说，在拍摄电影《冰刀双人组》时，我也遇到过这种要演两个版本角色的情况。

359
00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:43.500
Really?
真的吗？

360
00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:48.620
Yeah, I had gone in with this idea for this version of the character that was based kind
真的，我当时带着一个关于角色的构思去试镜，这个构思有点儿……

361
00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:51.500
of on like Bernadette Peters' performance in The Jerk.
借鉴了伯纳黛特·彼得斯在《阿呆闯学府》里的表演。

362
00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:52.080
Yeah.
是吗。

363
00:16:52.260 --> 00:17:00.900
I just really loved her wide-eyed, innocent, earnest approach to love and sex and just sort
我真的很喜欢她那种睁大眼睛、天真、诚恳地对待爱情和性，以及对待所有事情的态度，

364
00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:04.600
of all of it and being with sort of an oddball guy.
还有她与一个怪咖在一起的那种感觉。

365
00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:08.420
And that is very similar to my character of Katie in Blades of Glory.
这和我《冰刀双人组》里凯蒂这个角色非常相似。

366
00:17:09.080 --> 00:17:13.560
But I think there was a little bit of concern that that might feel like too big of a choice.
但我觉得他们有点担心那样演会不会显得太夸张了。

367
00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.460
And so they also had me do all the scenes just pretty straight.
所以他们也让我把所有场景都按正常方式演了一遍。

368
00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:22.140
And I did that for a couple of days, every scene.
我那样演了两天，每一场戏都演了。

369
00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.680
And eventually they went with my quirky version.
最终他们还是选了我那个古怪的版本。

370
00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:25.920
I was so proud.
我当时特别自豪。

371
00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:33.320
But it was super intense because I remember one day I was shooting the scene with Will Arnett
但过程超级紧张，我记得有一天我在和威尔·阿奈特

372
00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:34.100
and Amy Poehler.
还有艾米·波勒拍戏。

373
00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:37.160
They played a brother-sister ice skating duo.
他们演的是一对兄妹花样滑冰组合。

374
00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:38.500
Yeah, I remember.
是啊，我记得。

375
00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:40.420
And their little sister slash assistant.
还有他们的小妹妹兼助理。

376
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:41.660
Oh my God.
天哪。

377
00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:43.760
It was so much fun shooting with those guys.
和他们一起拍戏真的太有趣了。

378
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.580
That whole movie looked like a blaSt.
整部电影看起来都超好玩。

379
00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:45.940
I know.
我知道。

380
00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.300
But the cast looked like so much fun.
但演员阵容看起来就很有趣。

381
00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:48.860
We had a blaSt.
我们玩得很开心。

382
00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:55.520
But we had this scene and they were like, okay, we're going to lunch.
但当时我们拍那场戏时，他们说，好了，我们要去吃午饭了。

383
00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.980
Jenna, you get two takes.
珍娜，你拍两条。

384
00:17:57.740 --> 00:17:59.980
One in one version and one in the other.
一个版本拍一条，另一个版本拍一条。

385
00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.440
And I had to just nail both versions.
我必须把两个版本都演好。

386
00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:07.400
And I remember someone, and I can't remember who, came up to me early and they were like,
我记得有人——我不记得是谁了——早些时候走过来跟我说，

387
00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:12.000
just don't do a good job of the version you don't want to have them pick.
你不想让他们选的那个版本，你就别演好。

388
00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:16.120
Like, just do the version you like really well.
就是说，只把你喜欢的那个版本演好就行。

389
00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:16.600
Tank one of them.
故意搞砸其中一个。

390
00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:16.920
Yeah.
是啊。

391
00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:21.500
And then they'll be forced to go with, you know, your vision of the character.
这样他们就不得不选择你心目中那个角色的样子了。

392
00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:23.440
I couldn't do it.
我做不到。

393
00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:24.720
My, like, actor integrity.
我的那种……演员的职业操守。

394
00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:25.360
No.
没错。

395
00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.000
Just your whole moral compass.
这就是你的道德准则。

396
00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:28.240
You couldn't ever do that.
你永远做不出那种事。

397
00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:28.540
Yeah, I couldn't do it.
是啊，我做不到。

398
00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:29.220
I couldn't do it.
我真的做不到。

399
00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:34.420
It is interesting to me how characters become, like, who they are on a show or in a movie.
我觉得很有趣的一点是，角色是如何在剧集或电影中变成他们现在的样子的。

400
00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:38.200
This is reminding me of a project that one of my friends wrote.
这让我想起了我朋友写的一个项目。

401
00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.300
It was based on a real-life story from her life.
这是基于她生活中的一个真实故事。

402
00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.860
And she had this character of a guy named Ed.
她笔下有一个叫埃德的角色。

403
00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:48.060
And he was based on her friend in real life, Ed.
这个角色是以她现实生活中的朋友埃德为原型的。

404
00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:50.220
And Ed was also an actor.
而且埃德本人也是个演员。

405
00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:52.440
So Ed went in to audition for Ed.
所以埃德就去为埃德这个角色试镜了。

406
00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.360
So he's in front of the network.
他站在电视台高管面前。

407
00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.900
And he's like, hi, I'm Ed.
他说，嗨，我是埃德。

408
00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.180
I'm reading for Ed.
我来试镜埃德这个角色。

409
00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:59.340
And he just was himself.
他表现得非常本色。

410
00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.000
She wrote him as himself.
她写这个角色时就是照着他写的。

411
00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:04.180
We were all like, Ed's going to get the part of Ed, right?
我们当时都觉得，埃德肯定能拿到埃德这个角色，对吧？

412
00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:05.420
I mean, they wrote it for Ed.
我的意思是，这角色就是为埃德写的。

413
00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:07.800
And he didn't get it.
结果他没选上。

414
00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:10.580
I was like, how can Ed not get Ed?
我当时心想，埃德怎么可能演不了埃德？

415
00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:11.780
It's based on Ed.
这可是以埃德为原型的。

416
00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:13.120
Like, what?
这算什么？

417
00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:15.420
Lady, I have a very similar story.
女士，我也有个非常相似的故事。

418
00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:19.460
Okay, I auditioned for this lead role in a pilot opposite Dan Finnerty.
当时我为一个试播集的领衔角色试镜，搭档是丹·芬纳蒂。

419
00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:20.540
He's from the Dan Band.
他来自丹乐队。

420
00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:21.060
Yeah, yeah.
没错，没错。

421
00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:22.360
It was hilarious.
那太搞笑了。

422
00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:28.460
And when I read the role, I thought to myself, oh, this is so Mary Lynn Reiskub.
当我读剧本时，我心想，哦，这简直就是玛丽·琳恩·莱杰斯库布。

423
00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:34.460
And so I went in and I just did my best impression of Mary Lynn.
所以我去试镜时，就尽我所能模仿了玛丽·琳恩。

424
00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:35.800
I love Mary Lynn.
我喜欢玛丽·琳恩。

425
00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:36.420
I know.
我知道。

426
00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:37.720
I have worked with her many times.
我和她合作过很多次。

427
00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:38.240
I love her.
我爱死她了。

428
00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:39.140
I super copied her.
我超级模仿了她。

429
00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.080
I get to the final audition.
我进入了最终试镜。

430
00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.740
It's me and two other women.
现场只有我和另外两个女人。

431
00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:46.520
One of them is like a tall, gorgeous blonde.
其中一个是高挑迷人的金发女郎。

432
00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.580
The other one is Mary Lynn Reiskub.
另一个就是玛丽·琳恩·莱杰斯库布本人。

433
00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:55.860
Then I find out that Dan knows Mary Lynn and he wrote the role for her.
后来我发现丹认识玛丽·琳恩，而且这角色就是为她写的。

434
00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.140
Any wonder?
这有什么好奇怪的吗？

435
00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.900
Well, I was proud of myself because I was like, well, I guess I nailed it on what he was going for.
嗯，我当时还挺为自己骄傲的，因为我想，看来我确实抓住了他想要的感觉。

436
00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.520
He was going for Mary Lynn.
他想要的就是玛丽·琳恩那种感觉。

437
00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:05.920
Did Mary Lynn get it?
那玛丽·琳恩拿到了吗？

438
00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:07.380
No, neither of us did.
没有，我们俩都没拿到。

439
00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.020
They gave it to the blonde.
他们把角色给了那个金发女郎。

440
00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:10.240
Oh, no.
噢，不。

441
00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:11.160
I know.
我知道。

442
00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:15.780
And it was so funny because I thought to myself, well, obviously, they're going to go with Mary Lynn.
最搞笑的是，我当时心想，他们肯定会选玛丽·琳恩的。

443
00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.260
They're not going to go with me.
他们不可能选我。

444
00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:18.420
I'm copying her.
我是在模仿她。

445
00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:19.000
Right.
没错。

446
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:20.680
They didn't give it to either of us.
结果他们谁也没选。

447
00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.580
But the two of us were at least doing what he intended when he wrote it.
但至少我们俩的表现都符合他创作时的初衷。

448
00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:25.120
Yeah.
是啊。

449
00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:26.700
No, they went the other way.
不，他们选择了另一个方向。

450
00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:28.400
You never know.
你永远猜不到结果。

451
00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:29.220
You never know.
谁也说不准。

452
00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:31.060
Well, why don't we take a break?
好吧，我们要不要休息一下？

453
00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:33.800
And when we come back, we will really get into this episode.
等我们回来，再深入探讨这一集的内容。

454
00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:36.000
I have a lot of fun stuff this week, I think.
我想这周我有很多有趣的内容要分享。

455
00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:37.080
Me too.
我也是。

456
00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:40.500
I have some sunglasses information.
我有一些关于太阳镜的消息。

457
00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:41.720
Hold up.
等等。

458
00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:43.100
I have sunglasses information.
我有关于太阳镜的消息。

459
00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:43.960
Oh, my God.
天哪。

460
00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:45.880
What if we have the same sunglasses information?
如果我们得到的是同样的太阳镜消息怎么办？

461
00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:48.260
Whose sunglasses are you going to talk about?
你要讲谁的太阳镜？

462
00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.740
Two different people's sunglasses.
两个不同人的太阳镜。

463
00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:56.200
I have photo evidence of somebody's sunglasses.
